      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The core issue is that \"planting\" a deterministic marker in a live production consensus layer without trusting the update process itself creates exactly the vulnerability you're trying to exploit, as any undisclosed changes can be retroactively denied as backdoor logic. Publius guidance suggests relying on cryptographic signatures in public tools like Etherscan or Doxa to verify authorship, but tricking a committed corporation into a \"controlled update\" carrying your footprint can easily be met with a claim that their team never accepted or signed off on those specific line-level contributions, effectively nullifying the proof. The most robust path forward usually involves shifting focus from forcing an internal admission to creating an unbreakable public record of your actions or engaging third-party auditors who have absolute independence from their legal teams.",
